WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.564
First images of

00:00:01.376 --> 00:00:02.401
the impact of

00:00:02.213 --> 00:00:03.746
DART asteroid deflection

00:00:03.558 --> 00:00:04.477
experiment

00:00:04.998 --> 00:00:06.401
ESA's Hera mission

00:00:06.213 --> 00:00:07.097
has returned the

00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:08.453
first close-up images

00:00:08.264 --> 00:00:09.054
of the crater

00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:10.139
left by NASA's

00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:11.472
DART asteroid-deflection

00:00:11.283 --> 00:00:11.837
test

00:00:12.015 --> 00:00:13.229
showing the chaotic

00:00:13.111 --> 00:00:14.196
aftermath of the

00:00:14.007 --> 00:00:15.375
cosmic smashup

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.136
The first-of-its-kind

00:00:16.948 --> 00:00:18.622
experiment worked beautifully

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:19.707
as the solar-powered

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:20.921
space probe slammed

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.325
into asteroid Dimorphos

00:00:22.478 --> 00:00:23.657
permanently altering

00:00:23.469 --> 00:00:24.518
its trajectory

00:00:25.046 --> 00:00:26.461
The spacecraft released

00:00:26.272 --> 00:00:27.333
some 10 billion

00:00:27.145 --> 00:00:28.300
joules of energy

00:00:28.112 --> 00:00:29.114
as it impacted

00:00:28.926 --> 00:00:31.520
Dimorphos in September 2022

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:32.392
at more than

00:00:32.204 --> 00:00:34.916
13,700 mph

00:00:35.185 --> 00:00:36.529
shortening the asteroid’s

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.861
12-hour orbit around

00:00:37.673 --> 00:00:38.816
Didymos by about

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.830
33 minutes

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.364
Full story on

00:00:41.175 --> 00:00:42.071
the dashboard
